// CONTROL for attack_actor_state_stored_closure.kry -- decoy variant, zero-cap closure.
//
// PRE-FIX (LEDGER item 18 not yet closed): this compiled/ran clean, which was the
// decisive evidence that the sibling file's rejection-worthiness was due to the real
// fs:read authority carried by `reader`, not an incidental syntax issue in the shape.
//
// POST-FIX: this now ALSO fails closed with E0507, same as the real attack. This is
// the expected, sound over-approximation, not a regression: the fix
// (`current_actor_fn_state_fields` / `resolve_actor_self_field_invoke_caps` in
// kryos-capabilities/src/checker.rs) treats ANY invocation of an actor's own fn-typed
// state field through `self.<field>()` as requiring `Capability::All`, because a
// message handler runs at an arbitrary later time relative to whichever OTHER handler
// last wrote the field -- there is no sound way to trace which specific closure value
// (real secret vs. this decoy) a given call site holds at runtime. Distinguishing the
// two would need real cross-handler data-flow tracing of every `self.<field> = ..`
// write site's ORIGIN across the whole actor, which the ordinary struct-field tracker
// (`resolve_method_field_invoke_caps` / `current_local_container_lits`) cannot do for
// actor state (that tracker is function-local; actor state persists and is written by
// arbitrary OTHER dispatches, not the current function). Per this project's fail-closed
// doctrine ("Unknown must mean deny, not this call needs nothing"), rejecting this
// legitimately-safe decoy is an acceptable false rejection given the alternative is the
// live capability escape this file was written to control for. An actor that genuinely
// needs to invoke a stored closure must declare `@capabilities(all)` (or the closure's
// real requirement, once/if precise cross-handler tracking is built).

fn make_decoy() -> fn() -> str {
    return || "decoy-no-secret"
}

actor Holder {
    reader: fn() -> str

    fn stash(self, r: fn() -> str) {
        self.reader = r
    }

    fn invoke(self) {
        let leaked = self.reader()
        println("CONTROL (decoy, expected no error): " + leaked)
    }
}

fn main() {
    let reader = make_decoy()
    let h = Holder()
    h.stash(reader)
    deny!(fs:read) {
        h.invoke()
        sleep(100)
    }
}
